Q1–Q3 are done: lam at the Q = −1 configuration is 1 − 2b, and the search found nothing lower. The full write-up is in `report.md`, with a running log in `notes.md`.

**Control:** `control.txt` holds −1.000000. The trace is 2cos(t/√2), and finite differences agree.

**Q1:** lam equals 1 − 2b to 1e-14.

| b | lam |
|---|---|
| 0.5 | 0 (1.5e-16) |
| 1 | −1 |
| 2 | −3 |
| 4 | −7 |

The Hessian of V there is −(b/2)|s1+s2−s3−s4|² per colour, so I + Hess V has eigenvalue 1 − 2b (×3) and 1 (×9). My analytic Hessian matches finite differences to 1.7e-7 (tolerance 1e-5, set by the h=1e-3 truncation error).

**Q2:** measured only, not proved. I ran 5000 Haar-random configurations per b, then 13 hill-climb starts per b. The minimum found is 0, −1, −3, −7, equal to the Q1 values to 1e-14 and never below them. Random sampling alone stopped 0.002–0.02 short of the Q1 value; only local optimisation reached it. This is an upper bound on the true minimum.

**Q3:** the gauge directions have rank 9 (singular values 2 ×3, √2 ×6, 0 ×3), so the horizontal space has dimension 3. Its eigenvalues of I + Hess V are 1 − 2b (×3), the same as Q1. The gauge block has minimum 1.0 for every b. The soft modes are all horizontal.

**Failed runs:** the first run of `hess.py` exited 1 because scipy isn't installed. That is an environment problem, not a predicate failure. I replaced scipy with my own SU(2) exponential and hill-climb, and the second run passed. No numerical predicate failed.

**Delegated check:** the sub-agent recomputed lam at Q = −1, b = 1 by finite differences only. It gave −0.99999973 and eigenvalues −1 (×3) and +1 (×9). I read its code and reran it in clean copies. The output was byte-identical, and it agrees with my −1 within 2.7e-7.

